  "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B035",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the magic-wavelength principle and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B027",
    "B041"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B035 is a precise, mature achievement in which a fundamental control principle solved the decisive problem of confining many atoms without corrupting the optical clock transition. Optical lattice clocks now demonstrate exceptional systematic control and independently reproduced performance, making comparisons of atomic time a practical probe of gravity and fundamental physics. Katori and Ye remain the most defensible compact attribution for the originating magic-wavelength proposal and defining experimental establishment. The proposed wording deliberately recognizes that specific achievement, rather than the whole optical-clock field or prospective applications.",
    "case_against": "The remaining reservation is attribution: later advances in clock species, frequency transfer, and systematic evaluation involved many essential groups. We should therefore not make a generic award for optical metrology. An instrument-centered prize must also rest on the new physical capability already established, not on future discoveries it might enable. I judge that threshold met because the lattice-clock principle and its reproducible realization are themselves a durable advance in precision physics.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that the exceptional independently reproduced stability and accuracy of lattice clocks turn the magic-wavelength concept into a durable capability, while requiring a narrow boundary around the lattice-clock principle and realization.",
        "response": "I agree. This is the strongest reason to retain B035: systematic effects have not merely been reduced in one laboratory, but brought under reproducible control across the field. I adopt the narrower citation because it avoids crediting the wider optical-clock ecosystem while preserving the inseparable relation between Katori's key idea and Ye's defining experimental programme."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig prefers B027 because fluctuation theorems provide universal, experimentally tested relations for nonequilibrium systems, but notes uncertainty over priority among the related formulations and the absence of one defining experimental discovery.",
        "response": "I continue to regard B027 as a profound alternative; its experimental tests are important evidence that the relations are more than formal elegance. But the prize boundary still has to choose among closely connected theorems and diverse demonstrations. B035 offers a more singular chain from physical principle through exacting realization to independently verified measurements, with a clearer two-person attribution."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson holds that Cirac, Zoller, and Blatt established a mature regime of controllable atomic quantum information and simulation, while acknowledging overlap with wider trapped-ion and quantum-information programmes.",
        "response": "The controlled-atom achievement is scientifically outstanding and experimentally mature. Its breadth is also its present difficulty: foundational protocols, trapped-ion realization, optical-lattice work, and the wider quantum-information programme cannot easily be separated by the proposed citation. The lattice-clock achievement is narrower, but its physical core and credit line are correspondingly more exact."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical review can confirm that Katori and Ye alone encompass the indispensable conceptual and early experimental credit for the stated narrow scope.",
      "How explicitly the citation should name the magic wavelength while excluding later optical-clock, frequency-transfer, and application developments."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No recusal-triggering connection is apparent. My documented collaborations in attosecond physics do not involve the proposed laureates or the preferred achievement."
  }
}
